+Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index: each kiosk holds its own on-disk index file and rebuilds it from an imported snapshot. This keeps every lookup satisfiable from files already on the kiosk, with no service for the kiosk to run and no dependency on lab-network reachability. Backend identity, schema, and query path are private to this node; the certificates node sees only `findBySerial`.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build a new SQLite index from the candidate snapshot's records in a fresh file, separate from the active index, so a failure or crash mid-build cannot corrupt or truncate the index currently serving lookups. Activate the new snapshot only after validation and the index build both succeed, by switching the active index file in one atomic step; lookups in flight during the switch complete against whichever index they started on. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot's index active and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Query the active SQLite index by the normalized serial number using an indexed column, so a lookup across 90,000 records resolves without scanning unrelated rows. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index, rebuilt per kiosk from each imported snapshot | The HLD tenet that lookup must complete using only files on the kiosk, with no runtime network access, already rules out an on-site HTTPS directory and a managed search service — both need live connectivity this project's kiosks don't have during normal operation. The remaining cost, each kiosk validating and rebuilding its own index on snapshot import, stays entirely inside this node and doesn't change the certificates interface. |
